A||||8||BroCar 87||4 cyl. to a V-8 questions||||1||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||04-27-2000||09:12 PM||||O.k   JUST WONDERING but.... I can get a V-8 decked out with all types of mods for $1000. What is the BEST conversion package out there? I haven't REALLY looked, I have just checked them out. How many different ones are there?<BR>Also, this engine is a 383 from a 350. What do I do for a tranny. This will burn it up I AM SURE. lol   any suggestions will be appreciated. This may be better than just upgrading to the 2.8 or 3.1  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> huh!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-27-2000||10:15 PM||||Engine cradles from V-8 Archie to Gary Zumwalt cost $2K+ the last time I looked.  If you go V8 you'll need to beef up the axles. They can't take the additional torque.  The T125 auto is pretty sturdy.  I think John Norman's Northstar powered coupe is using a T125 auto.  I don't know if a 383 engine matches with a T125.  Was a 383 a front wheel drive engine?  Remember a Fiero uses a lot of components from FWD cars.  I've seen logitudinal engines in Fieros, which need a lot of engineering to put them in.  If you have plenty of $$, you can do anything you want.||||reg||9
Z||000002||KnightRyder31||04-27-2000||10:51 PM||||383 is a Chyrsler motor. It was put into FWD cars like the Dodge Challenger, Plymouth Barracuda, etc. That really sounds a bit to large to fit into the back of a Fiero. I would go with a 350, or 355. They do well in Fiero's.||||reg||6
Z||000003||batboy||04-27-2000||11:03 PM||||This 383 is a stroked 350, I'm sure. Old Lar, please don't tell my drive axles on the Batmobile they can't take the torque. As long as they don't know, they seem to do ok with my V8. The V8 Archie's kit is the most complete setup you can buy right now.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||04-27-2000||11:05 PM||||A Small Block Chevy 350 bored .030 over and fitted with a crank from a SBC 400 also happens to displace 383 cubic inches. This is a very popular method of wringing more torque out of a SBC 350 without some of the drawbacks of the SBC 400. This is what BroCar has, basically a poked and stroked 350.<P>Additionally, I don't ever recall seeing a FWD Challenger or Barracuda. Oldsmobile and Caddilac were the only manufacturers to build big V-8 powered FWD cars as far as I know.<P>I'm intrigued by the thought of one day swapping a 350 into a Fiero, too, but that day is still quite a ways off.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||BroCar 87||04-27-2000||11:16 PM||||It was a 350  they did some kinda mods. I will get the specs tomorrow. I don't know what car it came out of first but it was put into an Impala (96). I will post more tomorrow about the engine.  Just checkin my options here. I thought the 2.8 upgrade would be best but i found this engine in great shape with all but carb., and intake for near 1 grand so ::lookin stuff up:: Thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||04-28-2000||01:05 AM||||From all I've read, the stock Fiero axle's can take the torque with no problems.  Never heard of one breaking.  Just remember, anything will break if you abuse it!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||04-28-2000||10:53 AM||||I've only heard stories of axle snap when big engines were being put into Fiero using the stock axlels.  When the first attempts at putting in high torque engines into Fieros, there were junk yard runs at getting replacement axles.<P>Twin Lake Fiero is putting in heavier duty (modified?) axles in the 3.4 conversion he is doing for me.  Now this may be done because of the transmission modification, or because of the torque.<P>When was the last time anyone saw a FWD Plymouth Barracuda or Dodge Challenger??  That 383 Chrysler engine was the in between engine.  Between the Chrysler 318 and 426 Hemi.  They went in Roadrunners, Dodge Chargers vintage 60'2 and 70's. <P>The largest engine I've heard of in a Fiero was a 462 cubic inch Pontiac.  I think Zumwalt or Norman did it on a dare. I've only seen photos.  The intake for the carburators was above the roof line.||||reg||10
Z||000008||Spyder||04-28-2000||12:53 PM||||Ok,<BR>Here is my deal. I put a 355 cubic inch V8 in my 84 Fiero. I used the 5 speed out of an 88 for the extra gear. <BR>I used the V8 Archie kit.<BR>I guess the reason I bought the Archie kit was mainly because he would talk to me.<P>Now, the reason Zumalt uses the different axles is not because of breakage, (I know that’s what they say) they move the engine over two inches which is why you “Have To” have new axles.  <BR>I will most likely strip out every gear in the transaxle before I break an axle.<P>Do what you’re doing, ask around and do research and decide for yourself.  <P>Also FYI: The guys who make the V8 kits are in a WAR, heated exchange of words.||||reg||1
